  settings flow to modify, and the choices here (where data lives, what the read/write interface looks like, what a
  "session" is) get baked into every caller. So I'll work through questions, approaches, and a written spec rather than
  jumping to a short in-chat design. Say the word if you'd rather I treat it as a quick one-off.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m this repo has two independent runtimes, and "persists across sessions" means a completely different
  mechanism in each. Picking one now decides the storage backend, the API shape (sync vs async), and how it gets tested.

  - [1mBrowser[0m — preferences belong to the login page UI (remembered username, theme). Backend is [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m; the API can
    stay synchronous; testing needs a DOM environment. Cheap to build, but data is per-device and per-browser, and
    never reaches a server.
  - [1mNode[0m — preferences belong to the CLI in [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Backend is a JSON file on disk (e.g. under the user's config dir); the
    API is async or sync-fs; testing is plain file I/O with a temp dir. Also cheap, and easier to test, but irrelevant
    to the login page.
  - [1mBoth, over a shared interface[0m — one preferences module with a pluggable storage adapter (localStorage in the
    browser, fs in Node). Buys reuse and one mental model, costs you an abstraction layer and forces the API to be async
    everywhere so both backends fit. This is the expensive one to walk back if you don't actually need both, and the
    expensive one to retrofit if you pick a single side and later need the other.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser.[0m The only user-facing thing in this repo is the login page, and "settings persist across
  sessions" reads as a UI concern. The Node half is a [38;5;153mgreet[39m stub with no user to have preferences. I'd rather build one
  concrete backend well than an adapter layer for a second consumer that may never exist — and going browser-only now
  doesn't block adding a Node adapter later, since the module boundary is the same either way.
